---
title: Unit of work
description: Committing a transaction around a command, and publishing the events it produced only after the commit succeeded.
---

A handler saves changes; something has to commit them. Doing that in every handler is the work
an interceptor exists to remove — and doing it *correctly* means being careful about when the
events go out.

## The shape

One transaction per command, opened before the handler and committed after it. A query gets
none, because it changes nothing.

```csharp
public interface IUnitOfWork
{
    ValueTask<IAsyncDisposable> BeginAsync(CancellationToken ct);
    ValueTask CommitAsync(CancellationToken ct);
}
```

## Marking the commands that want one

Not every command needs a transaction. A marker interface makes the choice explicit and lets
the interceptor narrow to it:

```csharp
public interface ITransactionalCommand : ICommand;

public sealed record PlaceOrder(Guid CustomerId, IReadOnlyList<Line> Lines)
    : ITransactionalCommand;
```

## The interceptors

A pre-interceptor opens, a final interceptor closes. Two stages, because the final stage runs
whether the handler succeeded or threw:

```csharp
public sealed class BeginTransaction(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork)
    : ICommandPreInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand>
{
    public async ValueTask<ITransactionalCommand> HandleAsync(
        ITransactionalCommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var transaction = await unitOfWork.BeginAsync(context.CancellationToken);
        context.Set("uow.transaction", transaction);
        return command;
    }
}
```

```csharp
public sealed class CompleteTransaction(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork, IEventMediator events)
    : ICommandFinalInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(
        ITransactionalCommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!context.TryGet<IAsyncDisposable>("uow.transaction", out var transaction))
            return;

        await using (transaction)
        {
            if (exception is not null)
                return;                                   // dispose rolls back

            await unitOfWork.CommitAsync(context.CancellationToken);
        }

        await PublishPendingAsync(context);
    }
}
```

The final stage is the right place for the close because it runs on both paths. Disposing
without committing is the rollback, so there is no separate failure branch to keep in sync.

{% aside type="caution" %}
A final interceptor does **not** run after `context.Abort()`. An abort is a deliberate stop, not
a failure, and it skips the exception and final stages — so a transaction opened in the pre
stage would leak.

Either abort *before* opening one (put authorization ahead of `BeginTransaction` with a higher
`[Weight]`), or open the transaction in the handler where a `try`/`finally` covers every exit.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing events after the commit

This is the part that is easy to get wrong. Publishing inside the transaction lets a subscriber
act on state that later rolls back:

```csharp
// the email goes out for an order that never existed
await repository.SaveAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
await events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(order.Id), context);
await unitOfWork.CommitAsync(context.CancellationToken);
```

Collect them instead, and let the final interceptor publish once the commit has succeeded:

```csharp
public static class PendingEvents
{
    private const string Key = "uow.events";

    public static void Enqueue(this ErgosfareContext context, object @event)
    {
        if (!context.TryGet<List<object>>(Key, out var pending))
            context.Set(Key, pending = []);

        pending.Add(@event);
    }

    public static IReadOnlyList<object> Drain(this ErgosfareContext context)
        => context.TryGet<List<object>>(Key, out var pending) ? pending : [];
}
```

The handler announces without dispatching:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);

        context.Enqueue(new OrderPlaced(order.Id));
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

And the final interceptor drains the queue after committing:

```csharp
private async ValueTask PublishPendingAsync(ErgosfareContext context)
{
    foreach (var @event in context.Drain())
        await events.PublishAsync(@event, context);
}
```

Passing `context` keeps the publish inside the same logical operation — same items, same
cancellation. See [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Registering it

Nothing extra. Both interceptors are discovered like any other participant:

```csharp
services.AddScoped<IUnitOfWork, EfUnitOfWork>();

services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated()));
```

Order the pre stage explicitly if anything else must run before the transaction opens:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize         : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class BeginTransaction  : ICommandPreInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand> { … }
```

## When to reach for an outbox instead

Publishing after the commit closes the "subscriber saw a rolled-back order" hole. It does not
close the one where the process dies between the commit and the publish — the order exists and
nobody was told.

If that matters, write the events into the same transaction as an outbox table and let a
separate reader publish them. The interceptor above becomes the thing that *writes* the outbox
rows rather than the thing that publishes, and the shape stays the same.

## What this does not do

**It does not nest.** An inner dispatch shares the scope, so it shares the transaction. Nothing
here opens a second one, and nothing commits early.

**It does not decide isolation.** That belongs to your `IUnitOfWork`.

**It does not apply to queries.** They carry no marker, so the interceptor never matches them.
